From b9074b2d7a230b6e28caa23165e9d8bc0677d333 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Johan Hovold <johan@kernel.org>
Date: Mon, 17 Nov 2025 17:12:43 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] dmaengine: at_hdmac: fix device leak on of_dma_xlate()

Make sure to drop the reference taken when looking up the DMA platform
device during of_dma_xlate() when releasing channel resources.

Note that commit 3832b78b3ec2 ("dmaengine: at_hdmac: add missing
put_device() call in at_dma_xlate()") fixed the leak in a couple of
error paths but the reference is still leaking on successful allocation.

Fixes: bbe89c8e3d59 ("at_hdmac: move to generic DMA binding")
Fixes: 3832b78b3ec2 ("dmaengine: at_hdmac: add missing put_device() call in at_dma_xlate()")
Cc: stable@vger.kernel.org	# 3.10: 3832b78b3ec2
Cc: Yu Kuai <yukuai3@huawei.com>
Signed-off-by: Johan Hovold <johan@kernel.org>
Link: https://patch.msgid.link/20251117161258.10679-2-johan@kernel.org
Signed-off-by: Vinod Koul <vkoul@kernel.org>

diff --git a/drivers/dma/at_hdmac.c b/drivers/dma/at_hdmac.c
index 7d226453961f..22bb604a3f97 100644
--- a/drivers/dma/at_hdmac.c
+++ b/drivers/dma/at_hdmac.c
@@ -1765,6 +1765,7 @@ static int atc_alloc_chan_resources(struct dma_chan *chan)
 static void atc_free_chan_resources(struct dma_chan *chan)
 {
 	struct at_dma_chan	*atchan = to_at_dma_chan(chan);
+	struct at_dma_slave	*atslave;

 	BUG_ON(atc_chan_is_enabled(atchan));

@@ -1774,8 +1775,12 @@ static void atc_free_chan_resources(struct dma_chan *chan)
 	/*
 	 * Free atslave allocated in at_dma_xlate()
 	 */
-	kfree(chan->private);
-	chan->private = NULL;
+	atslave = chan->private;
+	if (atslave) {
+		put_device(atslave->dma_dev);
+		kfree(atslave);
+		chan->private = NULL;
+	}

 	dev_vdbg(chan2dev(chan), "free_chan_resources: done\n");
 }
-- 
2.34.1

The code statements that lead to the bug are:
kfree(chan->private);
chan->private = NULL;
Reason:
In `atc_free_chan_resources()`, the channel’s private data (the `atslave` structure) was freed and the pointer cleared, but the device reference taken in `of_dma_xlate()` via `bus_find_device_by_of_node()` (which increments the device’s reference count) was never released. This causes a device reference leak on every successful channel allocation and deallocation.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
atslave = chan->private;
if (atslave) {
    put_device(atslave->dma_dev);
    kfree(atslave);
    chan->private = NULL;
}
Reason:
The fix retrieves the slave pointer, then calls `put_device()` on the DMA device referenced by `atslave->dma_dev` before freeing the slave structure. This ensures the device reference count has been decremented exactly once for each successful call to `of_dma_xlate()`, eliminating the leak.

